In article <7488.9004201338@hilliard.ecs.soton.ac.uk> tjc@ecs.soton.ac.uk (Tim Chown) writes:

>What I was angling at was a method by which the same save file could
>be used on any machine, so in a multi-architecture network you could
>log in anywhere and continue with the same game that you had before,
>whichever machine it was on.  And maybe to have the same special
>levels and endgame on each machine too.  
>
>Just how much would the overhead be to store saved games/bones/etc
>in plain ASCII or whatever to give this compatability ??

	The big machine dependancies are:

		Structure slot offsets
		base type sizes (is an int 16 or 32 bits?)
		endian (is the high byte in an int first or last?)

	So for machine independant data files, you (a) can't dump
structures directly [write(fd,foo,sized of struft Foo)) won't work],
(b) assume that objects are always as big as they can be anywhere (int
= 32 bits, always), (c) write out small objects in an endian fashion
(most significant byte first). This shouldn't be too hard, you would
need to write I/O functions for each data type that dumped 1 byte at a
time, ie:

	#define MaxIntSize 64
	print_int(i, stream)
	int i;
	FILE * stream;
	{
		char output[MaxIntSize];

	#ifdef INT_32
		output[3] = (i & 0xff000000) >> 24;
		output[2] = (i & 0xff0000) >> 16;
	#else  
		output[2] = output[3] = '\0';	/* null pad 16 bit ints */
	#endif
		output[1] = (i & 0xff00) >> 8;
		output[0] = i & 0xff;
		
		}
		fputs (output, stream);
	}

The overhead would be a measurement of the time to move all the bytes around
and the space to store for the largest supported architecture.

